ible.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande at Alamosa.\n\n* WHEN...From Wednesday morning to Friday morning.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 7:30 AM MST Sunday the stage was 2.6 feet.\n- Forecast...Flood stage may be reached Wednesday morning.\n- Flood stage is 9.0 feet.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-06T07:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Alamosa  Vicinity/Central San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2dd215e378eb08e478cfc3dafdca53426c0e3e44.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2dd215e378eb08e478cfc3dafdca53426c0e3e44.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 8:09AM MST until November 3 at 10:00PM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"...The National Weather Service in Pueblo has issued a Flood Warning\nfor the following rivers in Colorado...\n\nRio Grande Near Del Norte affecting Rio Grande County.\n\nFor the Upper Rio Grande River...including Del Norte...Major flooding\nis forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande near Del Norte.\n\n* WHEN...From this evening to Tuesday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 7.5 feet, Major Flood. Water reaches base of Twin\nMountain Bridge on Hanna Lane Road west of Del Norte.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 7:45 AM MST Sunday the stage was 3.3 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 5.0 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage this\nevening to a crest of 7.6 feet late tonight. It will then fall\nbelow flood stage late Tuesday morning.\n- Flood stage is 5.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 6.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-03T22:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d4ed949559e4e9277fce312d5b5f78474443d089.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d4ed949559e4e9277fce312d5b5f78474443d089.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued November 1 at 5:57AM MST until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is expected to occur.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of south central Colorado, including the\nfollowing areas, the Eastern San Juan Mountains, and the Upper Rio\nGrande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\nRockslides will be possible in the mountainous areas. Highway 160\nand Highway 17 will likely be significantly impacted by this storm.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Heavy rainfall is expected to fall today and Monday across the\narea, leading to elevated concerns for flooding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe South Fork of the Rio Grande, including South Fork and\nMogote.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood http://cotrip.org","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.089a310d5f6b73825563e3a8cfaf9f90b6b00b53.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.089a310d5f6b73825563e3a8cfaf9f90b6b00b53.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued November 1 at 5:57AM MST until November 4 at 5:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall will be possible,\nespecially over the western half of San Luis Valley.\n\n* WHERE...The western half of the San Luis Valley, including\nAntonito, Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 PM MST this afternoon through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Runoff from heavy rain and melting snow over the San Juan\nMountains may cause flooding in the San Luis Valley, especially\nMonday and Tuesday as high flows move out of the mountains and\nthrough the Rio Grande and Conejos River.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe Rio Grande, including Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood http://cotrip.org","ends":"2026-11-04T05:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Del Norte Vicinity/Northern San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft; Alamosa  Vicinity/Central San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft; Southern San Luis Valley","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.089a310d5f6b73825563e3a8cfaf9f90b6b00b53.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.089a310d5f6b73825563e3a8cfaf9f90b6b00b53.002.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df